{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>How shall I curse — El has not cursed.</b> He says to me, 'curse' (אָרָה), which is greater than 'curse' (קָבָה); even in 'curse' (קָבָה) I have no power — all the more so in 'curse' (אָרָה). But according to Rashi, that 'curse' (קָבָה) is greater than 'curse' (אָרָה), it does not make sense at all that he began with 'curse' (אָרָה) and concluded with 'curse' (קָבָה). And it may be explained: 'How shall I curse — Israel did not curse El' — for the nations, when trouble comes upon them, curse their king and their god, but Israel are not so; rather, even if the Holy One, blessed be He, comes upon them with force and strength, as indicated by the name 'El,' nevertheless they do not curse, and they do not curse but bless even for the bad. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, also does not curse them even in the time of their corruption. 'And how shall I wrath — YHWH did not wrath' — for the nations turn YHWH from the attribute of mercy to the attribute of justice, but Israel never wrathed against YHWH, never provoked the attribute of mercy to turn it to the attribute of justice."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The Hebrew text alternates between two roots for 'curse': ארה and קבה; both are rendered 'curse' since English lacks distinct equivalents, but the argument about their relative severity depends on the reader tracking the Hebrew terms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:42.448168970Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}